\begin_layout Section
Abstract
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Background: 
\series default
The Mega2 software program has been available since 1/16/2000 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega2,_the_Manipulation_Environment_for_Genetic_Anal
ysis
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Additional analysis programs have been added over time, though this has
 required software developers keenly knowledgeable in the Mega2 internals.
 Currently, Mega2 converts data from several different genetic analysis
 data formats into the specific data requirements for over 40 commonly used
 linkage analysis programs.
 Recently, Mega2 has been enhanced to use an SQLite database 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://www.sqlite.org/
\end_layout

\end_inset

 as an intermediate data representation.
 And, bialleleic genotype data are now stored in a highly compressed form,
 much like that of the GenABEL R facility and the PLINK binary format.
 Meanwhile, the R community and Bioconductor community have developed a
 variety of genetic analysis programs complimentary to the programs available
 through Mega2.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Results: 
\series default
We have made it possible to load SQLite3 Mega2 GWSA databases into R.
 Accessing data frames holding these data for whatever purpose the user
 needs is very straight forward in R.
 In addition, we have made C++ functions for R to decompress subsets of
 the genotype data that are available in the Mega2 database.
 The extra memory is only occassionally necessary; the data is returned
 as a simple matrix.
 We created the base Mega2+R package that loads and manipulates data frames.
 We have also created several more R packages that illustrate how to use
 the data frames as well as perform useful functions: one to run the Bioconducto
r pedgene package on selected marker subsets, one to output data frames
 data as a VCF file 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/GenABEL/index.html
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and related files (for phenotype and family data), and one to convert the
 data frames into GenABEL 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://www.genabel.org/
\end_layout

\end_inset

 R 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/GenABEL/index.html
\end_layout

\end_inset

 gwaa.data-class objects.
 (Note: The Mega2+R program can be used to enhance GenABEL since it supports
 additional input data formats (such as PLINK bed, VCF and IMPUTE2); thus
 using Mega2+R allows these formats to be processed by GenABEL.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Conclusions: Mega2+R
\series default
 is a collection of R packages that lets users convert the GWAS data read
 in by Mega2 to data frames.
 The user can then to do subsequent manipulation of the data in R.
 The significantly memory consuming genotype data is kept compressed and
 decompressed to a nucleotide format on demand.
 We have found these packages useful and wish to make them available to
 a wider audience.
 Documentation for 
\series bold
Mega2+R
\series default
 can be found at 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://watson.hgen.pitt.edu/register/docs/Mega2R.html
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
