\begin_layout Section
Background
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During an association or linkage analysis project, one may need to analyze
 the data with several different programs.
 Unfortunately, it can often be quite difficult to get one's data in the
 proper format desired by each different computer program.
 Not only must the data be converted to the proper format, but also the
 loci must be reordered into their proper order.
 Writing custom reformatting scripts can be error-prone and very time-consuming.
 To address these problems, we created Mega2, which can be obtained from
 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://watson.hgen.pitt.edu/register/
\end_layout

\end_inset

 or via BitBucket 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://bitbucket.org/dweeks/mega2
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The early Mega2 could read input data in a few formats: LINKAGE format
 and Mega2 table format.
 Mega2 augmented both these input formats with additional information.
 For example, trait locus penetrance information can be specified as well
 as the allele frequencies.
 (Of course, the frequencies are calculated if not provided in a file.) The
 earliest version of Mega2 was written in the C computer language.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Over time, Mega2 was upgraded to the C++ computer language and some of the
 internals were rewritten.
 More linkage analysis programs were created (by others) and were added
 to Mega2.
 The data formats that GWAS data were provided in changed over time; thus
 Mega2 was enhanced to read data in PLINK format, IMPUTE format and VCF
 format.
 The volume of GWAS marker data increased significantly, so Mega2 was extended
 to support the compression of biallelic genotype data (though still supporting
 multi-satellite and other non-compressed legacy data).
 As the volume of genotype data increased, it became slow and burdensome
 to validity check the genotype data and generate allele frequency data
 for each separate analysis that Mega2 was invoked on.
 Rather, Mega2 was restructured so that the validation and metadata generation
 were performed once for a given data set and stored in an SQLite3 database
 file.
 Mega2 uses this database file, via a menu-driven interface, to provde the
 data needed for any particular analysis.
 The same database file serves as the input for all the different analyses.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Sqlite3 Database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Initially, Mega2 used C (language) structures to store its intermediate
 data before analysis; several alternative strategies were possible to dump
 this data for subsequent reuse without having to recompute, reanalyze,
 and revalidate the data.
 The first choice was to dump the raw structure data to disk.
 To re-read the data is possibly faster but it is much harder to inspect
 the data for debugging.
 Also, the pointers used would have to be relocated to new storage areas.
 A better solution, for a lot of reasons, is to create a SQLite table for
 each C structure and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

insert
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the structure data into the table.
 The data is inherently inspectable and there are database tools to help
 to selectively extract data.
 The data is portable, so it can be read on different platforms.
 Since interfaces from many languages are provided for SQLite, the data
 are accesible in many languanges besides C.
 Of particular interest is R because there is a wealth of existing genetic
 analysis programs already created, well documented, and available in both
 CRAN and Bioconductor.
 Also, researchers regularly use R to develop special analysis because it
 has been used in the past to develop statistical programs and has the resultant
 libraries readily available.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are tables for loci data, marker data, allele data, map data, phenotype
 data and genotype data as well as pedigree and person data.
 Appendix A shows all the tables and their fields if you care to study them.
 (The example section, coming next, will list the tables, in passing.) Here,
 we will make a few observations about the tables in general.
 The first issue was discussed previously: What to do with pointers.
 Where necessary a field was added to a table, we call a link field, viz.
 locus_link.
 This field is a unique monotonic integer.
 When the data are read back in, these links are looked up to find a pointer
 to use.
 Consider the locus_table and the allele_table.
 They both have a locus_link.
 The link in the locus_table is hashed to yield a pointer to the corresponding
 entry in the locus table.
 When the allele_table is read, the locus_link is looked up, then a pointer
 is added to a field in the locus_table (using looked up pointer) to point
 to the location of the allele_table entry.
 (These links are also very useful in R to perform the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

merge
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 function.
 For example, in the above case, to produce a table with rows containing
 both locus and allele data.) These links are also used to associate family
 with person data as well as phenotype and genotype data with a person.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Most tables are composed of integer, numeric and string data.
 The phenotype table is an exception.
 The normal trick of adding an extra column per row to indicate which phenotype'
s data is contained becomes more complicated because affection and quantitative
 data have different data types.
 Instead, the phenotype data for each person is one long byte vector composed
 of multiple values with 8 bytes of data per value either representing a
 quantitative value or an affective value.
 Similarly, the genotype data are a raw vector with a pair of bytes representing
 a genotype for non biallelic markers.
 For common biallelic markers, a raw compressed bit vector with two bits
 per genotype is used.
 (After the data frames are loaded into the database, you may select genotypes
 needed for your particular study.
 There is a function for a extracting a matrix of nucleotide notation data
 from the genotype table.) Note: the original compressed genotype vector
 created by Mega2 is one long vector.
 What is written to the database is split into separate vectors for each
 chromosome.
 The end of each vector may have 0, 1, 2, or 3 unused bit pairs representing
 no genotype.
 The Mega2 C++ program reassembles the vectors without these gaps.
 In addition, it can select specific chromosomes and/or specific markers
 needed for analysis and not needed to store all the bit vector data.
 The R version is not as sophisticated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rest of this document shows some examples of Mega2+R package use.
 Then we discuss the main package, 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
.
 Several additional packages, 
\emph on
mega2pedgene, mega2vcf and mega2genabel
\emph default
 are presented which not only accomplish the obvious function but also illustrat
e examples of how the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package and data frames can be used by other packages.
 After the example section, the implementation section discusses the highlights
 of each package.
 Note: the functions there are discussed at an overview level.
 If you need programming details, you should look at the CRAN package documentat
ion or look at the actual code.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is always difficult to share real data.
 We have chosen to use the SeqSIMLA2 program to generate a dataset for us.
 [SeqSIMLA2: simulating correlated quantitative traits accounting for shared
 environmental effects in user-specified pedigree structure, Chung RH1,
 Tsai WY, Hsieh CH, Hung KY, Hsiung CA, Hauser ER., Genet Epidemiol.
 2015 Jan;39(1):20-4.
 doi: 10.1002/gepi.21850.
 Epub 2014 Sep 22.] We will provide a small PLINK ped dataset using the simulator.
 If you wish, you can download the code from 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://sourceforge.net/projects/seqsimla/?source=typ_redirect
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 We started from the first example under the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

prevelance
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tab on the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Simulate by Disease status
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tab on the tutorial page on the 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://seqsimla.nhri.org.tw/prevalence
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 This simulation creates 1380 samples of 500,000 markers; a bit too much
 for sharing.
 But, a bit less than 200,000 markers are actually polymorphic while the
 rest are monomorphic.
 We chose to subsample the latter down to 1380 people and 1000 markers.
 These data will be used to illustrate Mega2 and Mega2+R operations that
 follow.
\end_layout

