\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 Package Primitive Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 function and tables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package function, 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
, reads the necessary tables from an SQLite database into a collection of
 data frames.
 It returns an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 containing these data.
 If the verbose flag is true, summary statistics will be printed as the
 data frames are created.
 Variables ending in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
_table
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, viz.
 person_table, locus_table, are raw data directly copied from the database,
 although some of the original fields are not necessary and thus not copied.
 Other variables in the R environment are derived information.
 The 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame collects data from the 
\series bold
pedigree_table, person_table,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 to produce the data frame equivalent of a PLINK .fam file.
 The 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 collects all the raw data bytes by chromosome from the 
\series bold
genotype_table
\series default
 for each person and makes a single new vector for each person.
 But the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table 
\series default
can introduce up to three 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

virtual markers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 at the end of each chromosome if the chromosome length is not an exact
 multiple of 4 (genotypes per byte).
 The 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
 contains the offset of each marker in the genotype data vector assuming
 all the markers across all the chromosomes are contiguous.
 We construct the corresponding offset for the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 and add it to the 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
.
 The 
\series bold
marker
\series default
\emph on
 
\emph default
data frame is a subset of the 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
 data containing the name, chromosome, position, original marker offset
 and marker offset for the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
.
 (Note: Creating the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 in compressed form with no 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

virtual markers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 would require a lot of bit manipulations in R which would not be terribly
 efficient while keeping track of the added gaps is not particularly hard.
 We could use Rcpp but why use it if we are using R.
 Of course, the Mega2 C++ program is written C++ and so does, in fact, just
 copy the bits it needs.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As stated above, we pull together the rows from the 
\series bold
pedigree_table, person_table,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 to make a 
\series bold
fam
\series default
.
 The 
\series bold
pedigree_table
\series default
 has a pedigree_link
\series bold
, person_table
\series default
 has a pedigree_link and person_link
\series bold
,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 has a person_link field.
 These link fields can be readily used by the R merge function operations
 to assemble data.
 As you might imagine, the 
\series bold
genotype_table
\series default
 also has a person_link field.
 The analog to these 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

_link
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 fields on the marker related data frames is the locus_link field.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
mkfam 
\emph default
and
\emph on
 setfam
\emph default
 functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package function, 
\emph on
setfam
\emph default
, may be not needed.
 The function, 
\emph on
mkfam
\emph default
, was called by default from 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 to create the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table.
 You may want to prune the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table to restrict the samples processed.
 For, example you might want to eliminate samples with unavailable case/control
 status.
 After you prune the table, 
\emph on
setfam
\emph default
 will set the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 It will also prune the 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 and 
\series bold
genotype_table
\series default
 in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to have only those persons left in the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now we get to the good stuff; its time to extract genotype data.
 We presume that we have adjusted the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table as necessary.
 The functions, 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 return a matrix of nucleotide pairs or encode integers with a row for each
 sample.
 They both take the same single argument which is a subset of the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 data frame.
 
\series bold
The marker 
\series default
data frame has two offsets that are used by the internal decompression code
 and it has a name, chromosome and position can be used to identify the
 data to the user.
 The two 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 functions are really dispatches that call into Rcpp code.
 They call the proper function for the level of compression: biallelic and
 more than two alleles.
 They also collect genotypes and alleles data from appropriate 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tables to pass as explicit arguments to the Rcpp code.
 For each marker specified, 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 will return the corresponding pair of nucleotides as a string.
 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 will return an integer for the first allele and an integer for the second
 allele; the two integers are returned as a single value with the integer
 for the first allele shifted 16 bits and or'ed to the integer for the second
 allele.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2 
\emph default
Package Marker Range Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We might have the genotype data to create a 1000's of samples by 1,0000,000's
 of markers matrix in R.
 But are unlikely to have the memory to support the tables.
 The genotype data is compressed, but we can not decompress it all at once.
 We introduce more functions to partition the set of markers into reasonable
 chunks.
 In particular, most probably we only want to process a chromosome or even
 some genes at a time; not all the markers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
setGenes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we can not readily process all the markers at once, what do we typically
 want to do is look at some genes.
 Presumably, we want to get all the genotypes associated with the markers
 of some collection of genes.
 We have imported the Bioconductor data Annotations: "org.Hs.eg.db"and "TxDb.Hsapien
s.UCSC.hg19.knownGene" into the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package.
 The former translates between a gene name or alias and the entrezID of
 the gene.
 The latter gives start position, end position and chromosome for each transcrip
t known for the each gene.
 You might want to use the data from another build available at Bioconductor
 or follow their instructions make your own mapping.
 The 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 function 
\emph on
setGenes
\emph default
 lets you supply alternate string names for these data.
 Mega2 will load these Namespaces from R when you access the 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 is the main interface to Mega2+R.
 You specify a list of genes to extract the transcripts for; then Mega2
 computes the list of markers that lie in each transcript.
 It computes an array of genotypes for each transcript from its markers
 and finally it invokes a call back function that was supplied as an argument
 to the 
\emph on
applyFntoGenes
\emph default
 function.
 The call back function will be called with three arguments: the first is
 a matrix of genotypes for all the markers in the transcript, the second
 is a data frame of the markers for the transcript, and the third is a single
 row from an internal data frame that defines the transcript (it has the
 at least names, ID, start position, end position, and chromosome).
 The call back function can apply whatever analysis is desired for the genotypes.
 It can also access the other data frames available in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 is intended to be the front end.
 By giving it named arguments, you can indicate the call back should be
 applied to each of several sets of markers in arbitrary ranges on chromosomes
 (ranges_arg =) .
 Or you can specify the entire chromosome to define the range of markers
 (chrs_arg =).
 And finally, you can just specify an arbitrary list of markers (markers_args
 =).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are several reasons that the officially provided ranges for the gene
 transcripts may not be to your liking.
 Some the transcripts for a gene appear to have the same start/end position
 as others but with a different transcript name.
 Some transcripts overlap others.
 The transcripts have one start and one end but do not account for non coding
 regions, promoters, etc.
 Perhaps you would prefer to specify names and for each name provide a start
 position, end position and chromosome to your liking.
 The 
\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 function records a range data frame that has at least the start position,
 end position and chromosome 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

observations
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 It also records a vector of 3 integer that indicates which observation
 variable in the data frame contains the chromosome, start position and
 end position.
 These range and selector data are used as the default for 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 has a call back function of three arguments just like 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
.
 With no additional arguments, it will iterate through each row of the default
 range, compute the markers contained between the start and end, compute
 the genotype matrix,and then invoke the call back.
 Alternatively, 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 may be given a range argument and a selector argument which will cause
 it to use the ranges supplied rather than the default.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 is the simplest function that uses the previously discussed call back function.
 It takes one additional argument that is a data frame of markers following
 the column layout of the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 data frame.
 It creates a genotype matrix for these markers and then invokes the call
 back function with the genotype matrix, the marker argument data frame
 and NULL.
 (NULL because there is no range information.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2pedgene
\emph default
 Package Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The CRAN pedgene package, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Gene-Level Statistics for Pedigree Data
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
