\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2vcf
\emph default
 Package Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This package serves several purposes.
 It shows you how to assemble data from the Mega2 database into more conventiona
l presentation.
 It allows VCF format files to be generated for R analysis that use this
 format input.
 Finally, it provides a regression for Mega2+R by producing the same results
 from the SQLite database that Mega2 can produce.
 All the VCF related files should be the same except for time stamps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You must call the 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB 
\emph default
function from the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package to load a Mega2 database and store the results in the global variable
 ENV, before the functions in this package can be used.
 This package takes the data that are available in the Mega2 data frames
 and writes them out into several files.
 The .vcf file is the main output; it is created from the genotype matrix,
 and that matrix is supplemented by info from the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table and 
\series bold
allele_table
\series default
 table.
 The 
\emph on
Mega2VCF
\emph default
 program uses the default argument ENV; if your environment is not stored
 there, you must supply the envir = argument to tell where the environment
 is.
 One interesting aspect of the code design is how it writes large numbers
 of markers.
 It attacks the problem a chunk at a time, where a chunk contains 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

X
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 markers (currently 1000).
 It generates the genotype matrix and related files for each chunk then
 append the results for these markers to the .vcf and other files.
 Then it reloads new data, builds a new chunk and appends it again.
 This looping strategy might prove useful for other problems where the data
 can be generated incrementally.
\end_layout

